		{"id":"11538245c3e3c3ac","type":"text","text":"# Use Pyvis to make professional network rule visualisation","x":7495,"y":3410,"width":705,"height":117},
		{"id":"2b1817c8b0282b87","type":"file","file":"01_Products/Layout_Gen_Design/Pipeline/Phase_04/Rules Visualisation.png","x":8360,"y":3135,"width":779,"height":667},
		{"id":"b66900d9e49c36ef","type":"text","text":"# Phase_04","x":9004,"y":1603,"width":750,"height":50,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d7674efd8c7b8d49","type":"file","file":"01_Products/Layout_Gen_Design/Pipeline/Phase_04/Phase_04_Plan.md","x":9004,"y":1916,"width":1200,"height":650},
		{"id":"2c9cac12fe7e0d6b","type":"text","text":"# Phase_05","x":10640,"y":1628,"width":750,"height":50,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ec6428e9d629d393","type":"text","text":"📋 **Rule Analysis for Your Project**\n\n**1. The Road Network is the skeleton:**\n\n- **Primary Road:** A ring road around the entire site. The setback from the boundary is 5m, and the width is 8m.\n    \n- **Inner Road:** A mandatory road around the Power Block.\n    \n- **Distances:** Roads must be a minimum of 3m away from buildings and 2m away from the Pipe Racks.\n    \n\n**2. Main Groups (Your 3 pillars for Step 01):**\n\n- **Power Block (Units 1, 2, 3, 5):** Must be located in the center. There is always a road surrounding it. The Main Pipe Rack connects to it.\n    \n- **Cooling Tower (Unit 12):** An important rule — it must be at least 50m away from the Admin Building.\n    \n- **Admin Building (Unit 28):** Must be positioned 20m away from the power units and with its back facing the wind direction.\n    \n\n**3. Utility Connections (Pipe Racks):**\n\n- The **Main Rack** has a fixed width of 8m, while the others are 6m.\n    \n- These are the lines that connect your blocks.","x":662,"y":360,"width":884,"height":536},
		{"id":"57b2adc02e434c3d","type":"file","file":"01_Products/Layout_Gen_Design/Pipeline/Phase_01/Phase_01_Plan.md","x":535,"y":1520,"width":1011,"height":400},
		{"id":"f233baec8c93a369","type":"text","text":"# Open Steamlit page\n\n### Quick Start Guide (Use Command Prompt)\n\n**Step 1: Open Command Prompt (cmd)**\n\nIn the VS Code terminal panel, click the downward arrow `v` next to the `+` button and select **Command Prompt**.\n\n**Step 2: Navigate to your project folder**\n\nDOS\n\n```\ncd 01_Products\\Layout_Gen_Design\n```\n\n**Step 3: Activate the virtual environment**\n\nDOS\n\n```\n..\\..\\.venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat\n```\n\n_(Make sure `(.venv)` appears on the left)._\n\n**Step 4: Run the application**\n\nDOS\n\n```\nstreamlit run Dashboard/App.py\n```","x":-520,"y":360,"width":928,"height":717},
		{"id":"14369d86ae2ca3cb","type":"text","text":"# Before Make Dashboard\n\n### 1) Python \nhttps://realpython.com/python-virtual-environments-a-primer/#create-it\n\n### 2) Streamlit\nhttps://docs.streamlit.io/get-started/installation/command-line\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rMLwiVrK3Fw&list=PLMi6KgK4_mk2rK5jD-BK5RigFIP2QSq8W\n\n**Что такое Environment (виртуальное окружение)?** Представь, что это отдельный «контейнер» или «комната» специально для твоего проекта **PowerPlan AI**. Все библиотеки (Streamlit, Matplotlib), которые мы установим, останутся только в этой комнате и не сломают ничего на твоем компьютере.\n\nWhere to keep?\nLayout_Gen_Design/              \n│\n├── 📦 .venv/                   ← Твое виртуальное окружение (создается само)\n├── 📝 Notes/                   \n├── 🐍 Core/                    \n├── 📥 Data/                    \n└── 📊 Dashboard/\n\nSet\npython -m venv .venv\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\activate\n\n(Если всё получилось, в терминале слева появится зеленая надпись `(.venv)`. Это значит, ты внутри!)\n\nDownload \npip install streamlit matplotlib pandas openpyxl\n\n### 3) Matplotlib\nустановили вместе с streamlit\n","x":-451,"y":1240,"width":790,"height":959},
		{"id":"dbd75cb2f074486d","type":"text","text":"## **What is the Dashboard in your project?**\n\nIt is your \"Visual Command Center.\" Instead of staring at dry lines of code in VS Code, you open the Dashboard (via Streamlit) and see:\n\n- **Interactive sliders:** You change the values of site boundaries A and B, and the geometry on the screen rebuilds instantly.\n    \n- **Viewport:** This is where your site's rectangle and the colored building blocks (Power Block, Cooling Tower, etc.) are drawn.\n    \n- **Scoring panel:** Next to the image, you see an evaluation. For example: \"Score: 85/100. Error: Cooling Tower is too close to the Admin Building.\"\n    \n- **Action buttons:** For example, a button to \"Generate 5 new variations\" or \"Export to CAD.\"\n    \n\n## **Why are we doing this gradually?**\n\nIf we try to write the code for 60 buildings all at once while factoring in all the rules from your Excel spreadsheet—your brain will fry. Our \"baby steps\" plan looks like this:\n\n- **Step 1:** Just learn how to draw an empty site rectangle in the Dashboard.\n    \n- **Step 2:** Learn how to draw the 3 main \"footprints\" inside it (our groups: Power Block, Cooling Tower, and Admin Building).\n    \n- **Step 3:** Teach the program to \"feel\" the distance between them (that exact 50 meters from your spreadsheet).","x":-520,"y":2280,"width":1055,"height":537},
		{"id":"f508b266d3094c8e","type":"file","file":"01_Products/Layout_Gen_Design/Notes/!Scoring_Logic.md","x":2040,"y":1860,"width":750,"height":900},
